WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.130
NATE KEATING: All
right, hello and welcome

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:10.487
to an introduction to MLOps
on Google Cloud at Next OnAir.

00:00:10.487 --> 00:00:12.320
I'm excited to share
this overview with you,

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.940
and I hope it will
be super valuable

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.200
as you continue to leverage
AI to transform your business.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.570
First, a quick introduction--
my name is Nate.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.840
I'm a product manager on Google
Cloud's AI platform, where

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.660
we have a mission to empower
every enterprise to transform

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.285
their business with AI.

00:00:28.285 --> 00:00:30.980
At Google, we've pioneered
a huge range of AI machine

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.890
learning breakthroughs, and
much of this industry leading

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.480
technology underpins
the products

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.842
we launch within Cloud AI.

00:00:37.842 --> 00:00:39.800
But at the heart of our
product decision making

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.930
is a focus on the
capabilities that will truly

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.080
help our customers unlock
new business value,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.680
and so our Cloud AMP
platform does just that,

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.990
by providing customer AI and
ML teams the infrastructure

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.750
and toolset to help
them get more done

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.110
more efficiently and
more effectively.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.110
Let's take a quick
look at the agenda.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.080
We'll start with the definition
and overview of MLOps

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.400
and the core challenges at play.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.150
We'll talk a bit about where
data science teams are today

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.980
and where we see
them going, and I'll

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.040
share a simple framework for
MLOps based on real processes

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.130
that we see in practice
both internally at Google

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.590
and from our mature customers.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.090
And we'll wrap up with some of
the services you can leverage

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.860
today to get started,
as well as where

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.110
we're making additional
investments in the future that

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.250
will be exciting to hear.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.260
So let's jump right in.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.220
MLOps formally defined
might be described

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.790
as both a culture and a practice
that aims at unifying ML system

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.160
development and operations.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.920
It takes both its name, as well
as some of the core principles

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.190
and tooling from DevOps,
and this makes sense

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.420
as the goals of MLOps and DevOps
are practically the same--

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.210
to shorten the systems
development lifecycle

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.940
and ensure high quality
software is continuously

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.730
developed, delivered, and
maintained in production.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.670
But machine learning has
its own unique challenges

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.160
and different needs that
require special attention,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.770
and so let's jump
to a slide that

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.830
may be familiar to many, which
is from the Canonical Google

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:10.100
paper 2015, "Hidden Technical
Debt in Machine Learning

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.460
Systems," the takeaway being
that production machine

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.830
learning is more than
training a model,

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:19.400
and that very little of properly
functioning ML systems is

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.363
actually model training code.

00:02:21.363 --> 00:02:23.780
There are a number of different
technologies and processes

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.960
that must be in place to get
the most out of production ML

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.370
systems, and it's really this
combination and management

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:35.630
of these different processes
that comprises true MLOps.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.750
A simple analogy might
be the assembly line.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.580
Prior to the assembly
line, automobiles

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:45.170
had existed for decades, and
even years before the Model T,

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.720
Ford had something
called the 999,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.790
which set the land speed record
at over 90 miles an hour.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.610
But it really wasn't until the
assembly line was introduced,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:58.300
which is also just a set of
technologies and processes,

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.850
that continuous, high
quality production

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.470
and delivery of
automobiles became

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.710
possible and inexpensive.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:08.082
And so at Google, the
Cloud AMP platform

00:03:08.082 --> 00:03:10.540
is quickly becoming the assembly
line for machine learning.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.220
But let's take a look at
where most teams are now.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.950
Most ML journeys will
look something like this.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:24.260
As a data scientist, it starts,
hopefully, with a clear ML use

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:28.280
case and business objective,
and with that use case at hand,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.740
you can start gathering data
from different data sources

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.410
and doing some
exploratory analysis that

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:38.420
helps you get a sense for
what the data actually holds.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.730
Once you have a sense for
what's in the data itself,

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.900
and you've imputed some missing
values and normalized your data

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.938
or done some future
generation, you

00:03:47.938 --> 00:03:49.730
can actually start to
approach the modeling

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.700
and figure out how you're going
to tackle some new experiments.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.580
And so you would
manually execute

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.470
these different
experimental steps,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.650
doing more data preparation,
more feature engineering

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.320
and testing, and then you
do some model training

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.500
and hyperparameter tuning on any
models or model architectures

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.340
that are particularly promising.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.440
Last but not least,
you would be evaluating

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.190
all of these generated
models, testing them

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:19.600
against holdout sets of
data that wasn't trained on,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.519
evaluating the
different metrics,

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.700
looking at loss curve stability,
and comparing those models

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.610
with other models to see which
one actually works the best.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.130
And of course, this whole
process is really iterative,

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.730
and it's manually executed
over and over and over again.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.330
You might begin by doing
multiple different concurrent

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.120
experiments, testing
out different ideas

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.550
and different architectures,
analyze and compare

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.560
the performance there,
and then kick off

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.890
new experiments based on what
you learn, until eventually,

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.140
you actually iterate
towards an optimal model.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.630
And you'll be
experimenting, again,

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.250
there with different
hyperparameters

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:58.610
as you tune that final
model, until again, you

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:01.220
get a model that is
sufficiently performant that it

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.680
passes all of your tests.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.213
And then, of course,
unfortunately, at this step,

00:05:06.213 --> 00:05:07.630
you put it in
storage and throw it

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.795
over the wall to the IT
and operations folks,

00:05:09.795 --> 00:05:11.170
and it will actually
be their job

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.212
to deploy the model to
production as a prediction

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:13.960
service.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.380
And they'll ensure all the
necessary features are served

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.900
in production, make sure
we have auto scaling,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.830
make sure we set up any
necessary deployment

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.500
pipelines for all the various
targets, which actually

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.740
may be in a distributed system.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:30.390
And so it's easy to see why
that process is difficult

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:33.070
and why organizations
are struggling here.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.190
First of all, it's
really time consuming.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.340
The steps are highly manual
and are written from scratch

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.040
for every use case.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.600
It's also inflexible.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.180
Custom built steps
can't be reused

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.240
and are only understood
by the author,

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.860
and so every additional
data scientist in your team

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.460
or in your organization can
easily leverage all of the work

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.800
that a particular
team member has

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.200
done towards their use case.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.720
And I hear from data
scientists all the time

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.880
that even they can't
understand the work

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.460
they've done six months ago and
leverage that for the future.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.520
This is also an
error prone process.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.160
You get issues like
training serving skew,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.510
where the lack of coordination
between IT ops and the data

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.840
science teams leads to
unexpected differences

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.240
in the online and
offline performance.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.790
Not only is this suboptimal
from a performance standpoint,

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:27.370
but it's also really hard
to debug and figure out

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.740
why that happened.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.040
In short, there is
a high marginal cost

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.410
for model development,
and it really

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.810
shouldn't have to be this way.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.730
And so let's lay out a
framework here and start

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.770
by addressing some
of the challenges

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:45.400
we saw using this framework
across the ML solution

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.700
lifecycle, and then can dive
into some of the specifics

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.020
and the details of
each of these steps.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.160
First, our first box on
the left, experimentation

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.810
is absolutely crucial.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:59.440
We don't want to hinder the
experimental process at all,

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:01.130
but want to lean into it.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:04.880
Data science today is
still very much science,

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:08.510
and for most use cases, requires
a lot of trial and error.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.350
Instead of cutting down on
the number of experiments,

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.580
we want a way to embrace
experimentation and let

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.190
ML teams track and manage all
their different experiments

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.680
they're running to make sure
they're confident in building

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.010
the best model.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.920
Second, rerunning
training jobs when

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.250
new data is available or
based on specific triggers you

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:30.440
might have set ought to
be really, really easy.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.920
We want a continuous stream
of new candidate models

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.320
to test out and challenge
the model running

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.350
and production to see if
it actually is better.

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:44.330
Third, models must be
properly tested, evaluated,

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.010
and approved for
release following

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:49.917
a rigorous audible and even
reversible CI/CD process.

00:07:49.917 --> 00:07:51.500
I think this step
is really missed out

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.100
on by a lot of data
science teams today.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.180
And lastly, for any model
running in production,

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.900
we want to always have a sense
of how the models perform.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.240
This is important to
ensure quality and business

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.270
continuity, but it's
also crucial for getting

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:09.550
the high quality
signals into how

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.040
to improve the model
for the next iteration.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.190
What better training data is
there than true production

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.860
inferences?

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.720
The dotted line
at the center here

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.780
roughly delineates the
training and serving

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.000
sides of this framework.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.650
Building a world class MLOps
team and platform is a journey,

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.240
and from a maturity
standpoint, we

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.030
tend to see that
customers find success

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:36.039
by focusing initially
on the left hand

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:37.840
side of this framework.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.100
The bedrock of MLOps is
reliable and repeatable training

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.670
pipelines, and so as we
go into the next section,

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:48.640
let's take a look at those
repeatable and reliable

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.660
training pipelines.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:55.870
First, we transform the
previously manual steps

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:59.340
of ML experimentation
into a reusable pipeline.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:02.800
You implement an orchestrated
experiment of various steps

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.180
as a pipeline, which can be
executed in its entirety,

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.460
and which allows you to
iterate by changing some

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.010
of the configurations to
point to different data

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.510
or use different
parameter values.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.250
And then you execute the
entire run as an experiment,

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:21.760
and it's this repeatable and
reusable training pipeline

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.220
that lets you run
multiple iterations

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:27.680
and actually produce
different models to compare.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:31.070
And though each pipeline here
may develop one or actually

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.460
many models, the
output of this process

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.190
is not a model to
deploy, but rather

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:40.610
the code of the pipelines
and its components

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:45.640
which is source controlled
and control repository.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.030
Then similar to any
software system,

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.530
you set up a CI/CD
process for your code,

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.550
including building components,
running automated tests,

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:56.490
and tagging all the
produced artifacts.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.280
Those produced artifacts, like
compile packages and container

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.340
images, are stored in a
central artifact store.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.340
And then the
pipeline is deployed

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:08.940
to a target environment.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.830
This can be a test or dev
environment, a pre product

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:14.400
staging environment, or
a production environment,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.045
depending on the CI/CD trigger.

00:10:17.045 --> 00:10:18.420
If this is a
production pipeline,

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.890
it is executed automatically
and repetitively to continuously

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.563
train your model
given new data that

00:10:25.563 --> 00:10:26.980
can be either based
on a schedule,

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:29.440
like daily, weekly,
we mentioned,

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.850
or based on a specific
trigger, for example

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.533
the availability of
new data or a drop

00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:35.950
in performance of
the model that's

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.650
actually running in production.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:42.010
When a continuous training
pipeline runs until the end,

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.630
it outputs a newly
trained model,

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:46.450
and that is stored
in the central model

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:48.820
registry for deployment
as a prediction service

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.090
at some point in the future.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.600
Crucially, the link between
the model artifact itself

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.850
and the training pipeline
is never severed,

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.520
meaning that all models
in the model registry

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.110
can tell you their provenance,
what pipeline trained them,

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.680
who created the model, who ran
the training job, what data it

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.140
was trained on and
evaluated on, what

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:13.190
were the metrics and results
of the different evaluations.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.930
This lineage tracking
is incredibly key,

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.660
as we'll see in the future.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.700
One additional thing
to highlight here--

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.520
you'll notice the parity
between the dev and experiment

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.910
pipeline on the top row here,
and the continuous production

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.100
pipeline on the bottom row here.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.800
Avoiding the dev and
production inconsistency

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.845
is crucial, as it's
the source of many bugs

00:11:34.845 --> 00:11:36.720
or underperformance of
models and production,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.065
and is really hard to
debug for most teams.

00:11:40.065 --> 00:11:43.250
So now that we have an overview
of the reliable and repeatable

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:45.650
training pipeline,
which again, is

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:48.170
the foundational
first step of MLOps,

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.121
let's take a look at
the serving pipeline.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:54.930
For deploying the
trained model, we'll

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:59.230
want to execute model serving
as a CI/CD process as well.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:00.930
So you'll fetch
the model artifacts

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.390
from the model registry where
our training pipelines have

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.040
actually stored
them, as we just saw,

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:08.680
along with the code that
wraps the model as a service,

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:12.730
for example, as a REST API, to
execute the following steps.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:17.050
First, building the prediction
service itself, then

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:19.360
running tests, which
may and probably

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:22.550
should be unique to your
use case and organization.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.060
And then deploying the service
to the target environment

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:27.280
or environments.

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.300
And it's during this
point in the process

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:31.790
where it's often advisable
to run additional tests

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.770
and add some release gates.

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.540
So for an online
serving use case,

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.890
you may want to canary your
model on production hardware

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:42.860
to ensure that it meets
certain latency requirements.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:45.860
You might actually also want
to test out different hardware

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:48.830
to make sure you're deploying
the model to the appropriately

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:52.870
optimal hardware for
that model architecture.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.860
In addition, you might want
to run organized A/B tests

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:59.280
to ensure that your new
candidate model truly

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.230
outperforms the current
model in production

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:03.410
before routing all of
your traffic to it,

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:07.140
and so you might take
1% or 5% of your traffic

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:11.460
and send it to the new model
to test out how it's performing

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.710
on true production data.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:16.470
For that, you'll also need to
capture the eventual business

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.690
outcome to serve
as the ground truth

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.740
in evaluating that
model's performance.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.550
When the model is deployed
in the serving infrastructure

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:30.840
itself, it starts to serve
prediction on live data,

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.200
and you can then enable
explainability and continuous

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.100
evaluation where the serving
logs are captured and stored

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.400
for monitoring and
analysis in the future.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.560
We monitor model
performance and production

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:45.050
to know if models
are going stale,

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:47.210
to identify if
there's any outliers

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:51.088
or if there's skew or concept
drift exhibited in the data.

00:13:51.088 --> 00:13:53.380
This would be something like
the statistical properties

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.390
of the data changing
over time, which

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:57.580
can trigger a new
iteration of model training

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:00.170
or experimentation, and
this happens all the time.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:02.110
We know we live in
a dynamic world,

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:05.610
and the data is constantly
shifting around underneath us.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.740
And so it's incredibly
key to keep a close watch

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.230
on the production
data that's coming in,

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:15.010
so we can identify, for example,
if a particular class is

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:19.350
seeing a shift in its
underlying distribution.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:21.700
Again, crucially, because
we have maintained

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:24.650
model provenance and
lineage like we mentioned,

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.920
we can use a model's
offline performance

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:30.430
as a baseline when
comparing against production

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:33.220
and quickly identify where
concept drift has occurred

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:37.210
as live data shifts away
from the training data.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.460
This also lets data science
teams quickly pull up

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:43.210
the training data and
pipeline to reproduce the run,

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.640
figure out where the
issue might be occurring,

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:46.740
and then debug things.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.000
Taking a step back
and zooming out,

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.250
here's the end to end
view of both training

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:56.820
and serving using
our MLOps framework--

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:59.590
a map to the input output
artifacts and different storage

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:00.797
systems.

00:15:00.797 --> 00:15:02.380
So we start again
with experimentation

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.540
and development,
where we develop

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:08.600
a training pipeline that
is reusable and repeatable.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.990
And then the code is checked
into a code repository

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.420
for source control.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:16.120
We then build that training
pipeline and deploy it

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.490
to the target environment,
either to staging

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:22.210
or production, using
a CI/CD process,

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:24.370
where the artifacts
are registered

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:26.500
in the central artifact
repository, where

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.480
we can pull out those
artifacts for deployment

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.860
or roll back in the future.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.090
Now, the pipeline is
running in production,

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.400
we can do continuous
training, either

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.940
on a weekly or
daily basis, or when

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.373
we have a particular
trigger based

00:15:41.373 --> 00:15:42.790
on the model running
in production

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.770
or new data is available.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.410
The outputs of these are
stored in the model registry,

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:49.810
which can be picked
up for a model CI/CD

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:53.270
process in the future.

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.610
That model CI/CD
process includes

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:58.850
all of the tests, stage
gates, and approvals

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:00.980
necessary to
rigorously make sure

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:03.950
that the model you're deploying
in production is ready

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:06.710
and is actually outperforming
the model that is currently

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.520
in production.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:11.280
And then crucially, when
that model is actually

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.530
running on production
hardware, we

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:16.200
want to make sure that
it's constantly staying up

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:19.260
to performance
metrics and that we

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:22.270
can identify when it's
starting to decay over time,

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:26.460
or whether there's skew or drift
so we can go back to an earlier

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:29.310
step in this process,
retrain new models,

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:32.970
and deploy them in
production for testing.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:36.237
In addition, this is a very
complex and high volume process

00:16:36.237 --> 00:16:38.820
where customers may be running
thousands of different training

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:40.733
experiments for
a single use case

00:16:40.733 --> 00:16:42.900
and testing dozens of
different models in production

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.390
at any given time.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:47.680
And it's important that we make
sure we get this full picture

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:49.960
and make it available
to our teams.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:53.950
And so each of our pipelines
and steps in this MLOps process

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.680
writes out key
executions and artifacts

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:01.030
to a central metadata store
where ML teams, IT leaders,

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.790
and governance folks have a
system of record where they

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:07.000
can do analysis or run
an audit to keep track

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:10.150
of these myriad processes
ongoing as we automate

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.980
the different steps
of our assembly line

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.690
from machine
learning [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:18.450
Now that we have this
big picture view,

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:22.420
and we've put it all together,
let's talk about MLOps on who

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:25.327
Google Cloud's AI platform.

00:17:25.327 --> 00:17:27.369
We'll discuss some of the
products and components

00:17:27.369 --> 00:17:30.460
that we offer to
enable such a system,

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:32.470
and as mentioned the
Cloud AMP platform really

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.860
is our suite of integrated
products for building and using

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:36.590
AI.

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:40.688
And here, we'll just take
a focus on [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:40.688 --> 00:17:42.230
Then we'll provide
a bit more context

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.990
on the relevant individual
products themselves.

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:49.440
So like before, let's
start with a team

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:54.430
that has existing data and
products and clear use cases.

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:56.640
They may be running
analysis and experiments

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.550
locally or in different
siloed environments

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:02.190
for different organizations.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:06.060
This is what data science looked
like sometime around 2010.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:07.800
There's a lot of
ad hoc analysis,

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.220
a lot of custom tooling, a lot
of different experiments run

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:12.270
locally.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:13.980
Not a lot of cloud usage.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:17.340
Laptops stored a lot of the
knowledge of data scientists

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.590
and organization, and when
they left the organization,

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:21.210
that knowledge was largely lost.

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:26.740
The first thing teams
usually standardize on

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:32.075
from this original view is the
core data science platform.

00:18:32.075 --> 00:18:34.450
This would be things like
which language they should use,

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:36.792
which frameworks they'll use,
the preferred development

00:18:36.792 --> 00:18:38.500
environments, all of
the different things

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:42.350
that most of the team members
will probably argue about.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.560
In 2020, the average
data science team

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:47.510
at most organizations has a
pretty good handle on this,

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:49.370
and though there are
many different options

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:51.370
to choose from, and there
are different benefits

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:53.752
and challenges associated
with those different options,

00:18:53.752 --> 00:18:56.210
and also there's a lot of low
hanging fruit for improvement

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:59.150
here, enterprises are
still better positioned

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:02.510
than they were five years
ago from a core data science

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:07.140
tooling standpoint and
model building standpoint.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.030
Harkening back to our
introductory section

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.660
and our framework, what most
teams are now moving to build

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:16.950
is this true production
grade platform for MLOps.

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:19.890
These are the services that
enable the end to end workflow

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:23.130
that we saw before following
our MLOps framework--

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:27.270
experimentation, continuous
training, model CI/CD,

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:30.070
and continuous monitoring.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.660
And so let's take a
look at what this looks

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:35.990
like for specific GCP products.

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:40.230
At the data science layer, we
have our AI platform notebooks,

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:42.410
which is a hosted Jupyter
lab environment integrated

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:46.070
with our infrastructure
and various services.

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:50.600
You can connect to a source
control system, run notebooks

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:53.360
in a scheduled manner,
and you have control

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:55.393
over the underlying compute.

00:19:55.393 --> 00:19:57.560
This is a familiar interface
for data scientists who

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.103
like to do ad hoc development
using Jupyter notebooks,

00:20:01.103 --> 00:20:03.020
especially the upfront
part of their prototype

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:06.460
and experimentation phase.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:08.530
In the MLOps and
infrastructure layer,

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:11.890
we've built this on modular
services backed largely

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:16.150
by open source, and mostly by
open source created by Google.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:18.160
Each of the boxes
here can be used

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:20.260
to varying degrees,
or none at all

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:23.220
depending on your
different needs.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:26.070
Based on many of the
internal expertise

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:29.200
we have and working with
many of our customers,

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:32.120
we know that AI workloads
can be highly customized,

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.430
and so our services are designed
for novice, intermediate,

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.380
and advanced machine learning
teams to use whatever they need

00:20:38.380 --> 00:20:41.970
and also bring their own tools
to the platform, as well.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:46.790
It's the core of this MLOps
layer lives our AI platform

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.230
training and
prediction services,

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:53.850
which are the backbone of
building and running models.

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:55.290
The items in green
are where we're

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:57.480
making our latest investments--

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:01.770
a feature store to help solve
training serving skew issues

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:04.260
and improved collaboration
within teams.

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:08.545
By registering features during
the upfront training process,

00:21:08.545 --> 00:21:10.170
you can ensure that
those same features

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:14.130
are available in production
for your model at low latency.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:16.530
And you can also share those
features with other team

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:20.340
members to ensure that all teams
have access to the great work

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:23.040
that individual data
scientists are doing

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:25.020
and make sure we're
not duplicating effort,

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.410
and we're using the most
powerful and effective features

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:31.000
to build new models.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:34.560
In addition, a robust
experiment tracking service

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.580
to enable large scale
experimentation--

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:41.760
the ML model introspection
use case and comparison

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:44.010
incorporating great Google
open source products

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:45.720
like TensorBoard.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:48.210
This is also really
critical because the amount

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:50.730
of experiments we
run is directly

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:53.370
proportional to how confident
we are that we've really

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.650
chosen the best model.

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:58.440
By making experiments shareable
and easily referenceable

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:01.590
in the future, we can
then enable other members

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.810
of our team working on the
same or similar use cases

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:05.185
to learn from the
work that we've

00:22:05.185 --> 00:22:10.530
done in generating the optimal
model for our use case.

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:13.810
At the bottom layer there,
we have our ML pipelines

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:17.200
and centralized metadata store
for orchestrated managed end

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.230
to end workflows.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.240
These are really critical
for building this end

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:24.390
to end capability and making
sure we're not writing custom

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:27.560
code for different
steps of this end

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:29.970
to end process that can't
be leveraged and reused

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:31.030
in the future.

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:33.750
We want to make sure we're
orchestrating full runs of end

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:37.680
to end pipelines and tracking
the outputs of each step,

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:39.690
the inputs and
outputs of each step,

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:42.232
in our central metadata store.

00:22:42.232 --> 00:22:44.190
And for many of these
different items in green,

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:47.760
we offer great open source
and hosted solutions today,

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:49.950
and we're working on
bringing managed services

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:53.740
to these areas in 2020.

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:56.860
And so what's shown here is
simply the high level overview

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:59.435
focused on our MLOps stack.

